1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,133
Three kilometres beneath the floor of the North
Sea lies a layer of sandstone.

2
00:00:08,133 --> 00:00:13,069
Its pores are filled with oil and gas, trapped
there for millions of years.

3
00:00:13,489 --> 00:00:22,100
To reach it, we have to drill a hole barely wider than a dinner plate, through
hundreds of metres of seawater, and kilometres of solid rock.

4
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:27,196
We have to do it without ever losing control of
the enormous pressures down there.

5
00:00:27,616 --> 00:00:34,049
And when the well has done its job, decades later, we have to
seal it so well that it stays sealed... forever.

6
00:00:35,069 --> 00:00:41,711
In this video, we'll follow one subsea well on the Norwegian
Continental Shelf through its entire life.

7
00:00:42,131 --> 00:00:43,259
How it's designed.

8
00:00:43,259 --> 00:00:44,325
How it's drilled.

9
00:00:44,325 --> 00:00:45,516
How it's completed.

10
00:00:45,516 --> 00:00:48,274
And finally, how it's plugged and abandoned.

11
00:00:52,808 --> 00:00:53,955
Let's set the scene.

12
00:00:54,675 --> 00:01:01,020
Our well sits in about three hundred and fifty metres of water, and
nothing of it will ever stick up above the waves.

13
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:07,536
Instead, everything, from the wellhead to the valves that
control the flow, sits on the seabed.

14
00:01:07,536 --> 00:01:14,016
The oil and gas travel through pipelines to a production
facility, which can be many kilometres away.

15
00:01:14,016 --> 00:01:16,262
That's what makes it a subsea well.

16
00:01:17,182 --> 00:01:22,570
Our target is a sandstone reservoir, about three
kilometres below the seabed.

17
00:01:22,570 --> 00:01:27,889
Its pores, the tiny spaces between the grains of
sand, hold the oil and gas.

18
00:01:28,309 --> 00:01:33,833
And sitting right on top of it is a layer of
dense, impermeable shale: the cap rock.

19
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:36,924
It's the reason the oil and gas are still here.

20
00:01:36,924 --> 00:01:40,343
For millions of years, it has been nature's own
lid.

21
00:01:40,763 --> 00:01:42,220
Keep that idea in mind.

22
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:47,414
Because at the very end of this well's life, our
job will be to put that lid back.

23
00:01:49,725 --> 00:01:54,779
Before a single metre is drilled, the well is
designed in painstaking detail.

24
00:01:54,779 --> 00:01:58,717
And almost every decision comes down to one
thing: pressure.

25
00:01:59,537 --> 00:02:01,753
The fluids in the rock are under pressure.

26
00:02:01,753 --> 00:02:03,283
We call it the pore pressure.

27
00:02:03,703 --> 00:02:10,671
If the pressure in our wellbore ever falls below it, oil, gas or
water will start pushing their way into the well.

28
00:02:10,671 --> 00:02:11,955
That's called a kick.

29
00:02:11,955 --> 00:02:15,256
And a kick that isn't controlled can become a
blowout.

30
00:02:16,276 --> 00:02:20,154
So we keep the well full of a heavy fluid called
drilling mud.

31
00:02:20,154 --> 00:02:27,473
The pressure at the bottom of a column of fluid is simply its
density, times gravity, times the height of the column.

32
00:02:27,893 --> 00:02:36,790
Take mud one and a half times as dense as water, three thousand metres deep, and
the pressure at the bottom is about four hundred and forty bar.

33
00:02:36,790 --> 00:02:40,127
That's like balancing a grand piano on your
fingertip.

34
00:02:41,347 --> 00:02:44,533
But we can't just make the mud as heavy as
possible.

35
00:02:44,533 --> 00:02:47,289
Push too hard, and we'll crack the rock open.

36
00:02:47,289 --> 00:02:49,066
That's the fracture pressure.

37
00:02:49,066 --> 00:02:55,498
Mud pours away into the formation, the level in the well
drops... and that can trigger a kick of its own.

38
00:02:56,518 --> 00:03:03,092
So the mud pressure must always stay inside a window: above the
pore pressure, but below the fracture pressure.

39
00:03:03,512 --> 00:03:09,970
Plot both against depth, and you get the single most important
chart in well design: the drilling window.

40
00:03:13,475 --> 00:03:14,962
Now here's the problem.

41
00:03:14,962 --> 00:03:17,159
As we go deeper, the window moves.

42
00:03:18,379 --> 00:03:24,384
Sooner or later, the mud weight we need at the bottom of the
hole would fracture the weaker rock higher up.

43
00:03:24,804 --> 00:03:32,377
The solution is to seal off the hole we've drilled so far with
steel pipe, called casing, cemented firmly in place.

44
00:03:32,377 --> 00:03:35,999
Then we carry on with a smaller bit, and a
heavier mud.

45
00:03:37,019 --> 00:03:42,313
Repeat that, step by step, and you get the
classic telescope shape of an offshore well.

46
00:03:43,133 --> 00:03:53,789
30" conductor · 20" surface casing · 13⅜" · 9⅝"
· 7" liner

47
00:03:55,809 --> 00:03:59,017
Every string is engineered for three load cases.

48
00:03:59,017 --> 00:04:00,889
Burst, from pressure inside.

49
00:04:00,889 --> 00:04:03,028
Collapse, from pressure outside.

50
00:04:03,028 --> 00:04:08,241
And tension, from its own enormous weight, which
can reach hundreds of tonnes.

51
00:04:10,975 --> 00:04:22,803
Under the Norwegian standard NORSOK D-010, there must always be two independent,
tested barriers between the reservoir and the outside world.

52
00:04:23,223 --> 00:04:25,243
If one fails, the other one holds.

53
00:04:26,063 --> 00:04:31,725
We draw them as two envelopes: the primary barrier in
blue, and the secondary barrier in red.

54
00:04:31,725 --> 00:04:35,560
And we'll keep checking them at every stage of
the well's life.

55
00:04:39,083 --> 00:04:41,608
With the design approved, it's time to drill.

56
00:04:42,628 --> 00:04:54,479
The job is done by a semi-submersible drilling rig: a floating platform standing on huge
pontoons, ballasted down below the waves so that it barely notices the North Sea swell.

57
00:04:54,899 --> 00:04:59,920
It's held in position above the well by anchors,
or by computer-controlled thrusters.

58
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:02,150
At its heart is the derrick.

59
00:05:02,150 --> 00:05:11,138
Here, the top drive rotates the drill string: a chain of steel pipe, screwed
together length by length, until it's several kilometres long.

60
00:05:13,850 --> 00:05:18,879
The first sections are drilled riserless, with
no pipe connecting the well to the rig.

61
00:05:19,299 --> 00:05:26,838
A thirty-six inch hole is drilled into the soft sediments, and seawater
and rock cuttings simply spill out onto the seabed.

62
00:05:28,058 --> 00:05:30,767
The conductor is run and cemented in place.

63
00:05:30,767 --> 00:05:36,689
It carries the low-pressure wellhead housing,
and forms the structural foundation of the well.

64
00:05:37,709 --> 00:05:44,893
Next comes a twenty-six inch hole, and the twenty-inch surface
casing, crowned by the high-pressure wellhead housing.

65
00:05:44,893 --> 00:05:48,147
This is the anchor point for everything that
follows.

66
00:05:50,850 --> 00:05:58,359
Before drilling any deeper, into rock that could hold pressure, we
need our safety net: the blowout preventer, or BOP.

67
00:05:58,779 --> 00:06:06,650
It's a stack of massive hydraulic valves, around fifteen metres tall and
weighing several hundred tonnes, latched onto the wellhead.

68
00:06:07,470 --> 00:06:12,563
Annular preventers can squeeze a rubber seal
around almost anything in the hole.

69
00:06:12,563 --> 00:06:15,492
Pipe rams close tightly around the drill pipe.

70
00:06:16,512 --> 00:06:23,346
And as a last line of defence, the blind shear rams can cut
straight through the pipe, and seal the well completely.

71
00:06:24,966 --> 00:06:33,019
On top sits the marine riser: a wide steel pipe running all the way
up to the rig, so the mud can return to the surface.

72
00:06:35,558 --> 00:06:39,782
Now the real drilling begins, and the mud
becomes the star of the show.

73
00:06:40,502 --> 00:06:44,532
Powerful pumps push it down through the inside
of the drill string.

74
00:06:44,532 --> 00:06:49,826
It jets out through nozzles in the bit, cooling
it, and flushing away the rock cuttings.

75
00:06:50,246 --> 00:06:57,544
Then it carries those cuttings back up the annulus, the gap between the
drill string and the wall, and up the riser to the rig.

76
00:06:57,964 --> 00:07:04,152
There, shale shakers sieve out the rock, and the cleaned mud
goes back to the pits, ready to go round again.

77
00:07:05,372 --> 00:07:13,834
The bit itself is usually a PDC bit, studded with synthetic diamond
cutters that shear the rock like a plane shaving wood.

78
00:07:15,054 --> 00:07:24,401
Just behind it, sensors measure the well's direction and the properties of the rock, and
send their data to the surface as pressure pulses in the mud itself.

79
00:07:25,421 --> 00:07:32,754
And with a rotary steerable system, the driller can bend the well,
gently, towards a target that may be kilometres away.

80
00:07:35,767 --> 00:07:39,021
Section by section, the well goes deeper.

81
00:07:39,021 --> 00:07:39,497
Drill.

82
00:07:39,497 --> 00:07:40,370
Run casing.

83
00:07:40,370 --> 00:07:40,925
Cement.

84
00:07:40,925 --> 00:07:41,322
Test.

85
00:07:42,342 --> 00:07:45,597
Every cement job follows the same choreography.

86
00:07:45,597 --> 00:07:53,352
First a bottom plug, then the cement, then a top plug, and
finally mud, to push the whole train down the casing.

87
00:07:54,372 --> 00:07:57,043
When the top plug lands, the pressure spikes.

88
00:07:57,043 --> 00:07:58,350
We've bumped the plug.

89
00:07:58,350 --> 00:08:03,455
The cement has been squeezed up into the
annulus, where it sets hard and seals it off.

90
00:08:04,675 --> 00:08:13,251
Then, after drilling a few metres of new hole, a leak-off test checks that the
rock below the casing shoe can take the heavier mud to come.

91
00:08:15,767 --> 00:08:17,385
And if a kick does happen?

92
00:08:17,385 --> 00:08:20,311
The first warning usually comes at the surface.

93
00:08:20,311 --> 00:08:22,427
The mud pits start to gain volume.

94
00:08:23,647 --> 00:08:32,820
The crew shuts in the well with the BOP, reads the pressures, and circulates the
influx out through the choke line, while pumping in heavier kill mud.

95
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:36,278
The well is under control again.

96
00:08:36,278 --> 00:08:39,590
And that is exactly why we always keep two
barriers.

97
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:47,530
Once the reservoir section is drilled, the well
is still just a hole lined with steel.

98
00:08:47,530 --> 00:08:49,803
Completion turns it into a producer.

99
00:08:50,623 --> 00:08:54,639
First, the seven-inch liner is cemented across
the reservoir.

100
00:08:54,639 --> 00:08:58,458
To connect the well to the rock, we fire
perforating guns.

101
00:08:59,478 --> 00:09:08,307
Each shaped charge fires a jet of metal at around seven kilometres per second,
punching through steel and cement, and deep into the reservoir.

102
00:09:09,927 --> 00:09:11,956
Next comes the upper completion.

103
00:09:11,956 --> 00:09:16,837
The production tubing is the pipe the oil and
gas will actually flow through.

104
00:09:17,257 --> 00:09:21,204
A production packer seals the gap between the
tubing and the casing.

105
00:09:22,424 --> 00:09:28,895
A downhole safety valve, a few hundred metres below the
seabed, is held open by hydraulic pressure.

106
00:09:28,895 --> 00:09:32,098
Lose that pressure, and it snaps shut on its
own.

107
00:09:33,518 --> 00:09:44,147
The BOP and riser are then pulled, and in their place we install the subsea christmas
tree: a compact assembly of valves that controls the flow from the well.

108
00:09:45,367 --> 00:09:53,734
It's tied in to the production system with flowlines, and an umbilical
supplies it with hydraulic power, electricity, and chemicals.

109
00:09:54,954 --> 00:09:58,239
The well is cleaned up, tested, and handed over.

110
00:09:58,239 --> 00:09:59,950
Two barriers, once again.

111
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:02,962
The tubing, packer and safety valve in blue.

112
00:10:02,962 --> 00:10:06,042
The casing, cement, wellhead and tree in red.

113
00:10:09,342 --> 00:10:12,351
For the next twenty or thirty years, the well
produces.

114
00:10:13,371 --> 00:10:21,490
The reservoir pressure slowly falls, more and more water comes with the
oil, and eventually, every well reaches the end of its life.

115
00:10:22,510 --> 00:10:24,361
And then comes the final chapter.

116
00:10:27,092 --> 00:10:33,392
On the Norwegian shelf, plugging and abandonment, or
P&A, comes with a remarkable requirement.

117
00:10:33,392 --> 00:10:36,877
The well must be sealed with an eternal
perspective.

118
00:10:37,297 --> 00:10:38,279
Not for years.

119
00:10:38,279 --> 00:10:39,400
Not for decades.

120
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:41,574
For as long as the rock itself.

121
00:10:42,794 --> 00:10:47,880
First, the well is killed with heavy fluid to
balance the reservoir pressure.

122
00:10:47,880 --> 00:10:53,890
The christmas tree is removed, a BOP is installed once
again, and the tubing is pulled out.

123
00:10:55,110 --> 00:10:58,973
Just like during drilling, we need two
independent barriers.

124
00:10:58,973 --> 00:11:06,570
But this time they're permanent, and each one must seal the entire
cross-section of the well, including every annulus.

125
00:11:06,570 --> 00:11:07,407
Rock to rock.

126
00:11:08,627 --> 00:11:10,726
And that's where it gets tricky.

127
00:11:10,726 --> 00:11:15,579
Behind the casing, the original cement may be
missing, or of poor quality.

128
00:11:15,579 --> 00:11:19,777
A plug inside the pipe won't stop fluid sneaking
up the outside.

129
00:11:20,797 --> 00:11:29,358
So the casing is either milled away completely, or perforated, washed, and
cemented, so the new cement bonds straight to the formation.

130
00:11:30,778 --> 00:11:38,899
The primary barrier is set just above the reservoir, in the cap rock,
where the rock is strong enough to hold the pressure below.

131
00:11:38,899 --> 00:11:41,039
The secondary barrier backs it up.

132
00:11:41,039 --> 00:11:45,194
Each one is a plug of solid cement, typically
tens of metres long.

133
00:11:46,614 --> 00:11:51,189
Near the seabed, a final surface plug seals the
well off from the ocean.

134
00:11:52,409 --> 00:11:56,202
Every plug is verified: tagged with weight, and
pressure tested.

135
00:11:57,422 --> 00:12:03,421
Finally, the casings are cut a few metres below the seabed,
and the wellhead is lifted to the surface.

136
00:12:05,041 --> 00:12:07,347
What's left is... just the seabed.

137
00:12:07,347 --> 00:12:09,450
The cap rock has been restored.

138
00:12:09,450 --> 00:12:11,485
Nature's lid is back in place.

139
00:12:15,517 --> 00:12:18,085
So, let's recap the life of a subsea well.

140
00:12:19,005 --> 00:12:25,101
It's designed around the drilling window, with a telescope
of casings, and two barriers at all times.

141
00:12:26,121 --> 00:12:32,206
It's drilled from a floating rig, through a blowout preventer,
with mud holding back the pressure of the rock.

142
00:12:33,226 --> 00:12:38,673
It's completed with a liner, perforations,
tubing, a safety valve, and a christmas tree.

143
00:12:39,693 --> 00:12:45,891
And at the end of its life, it's sealed with permanent,
rock-to-rock barriers, and removed without a trace.

144
00:12:47,111 --> 00:12:50,465
Decades of engineering, all for one hole in the
ground.

145
00:12:50,465 --> 00:12:51,684
Thanks for watching.

